title: CMV prevention and treatment after allogeneic transplantation
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

''Adult allo-HCT:'' Prevent CMV disease with letermovir plus surveillance; individualize treatment by kinetics, immunosuppression, and toxicity.

!! Definitions

* ''DNAemia:'' CMV DNA detected in blood; neither proves productive replication nor organ disease.
** Reactivation represents renewed activity of latent infection in a previously infected recipient.
* ''Preemptive therapy (PET):'' Treat significant asymptomatic DNAemia to prevent clinical disease.
* ''Disease treatment:'' Treat symptomatic organ involvement supported by site-specific evidence, usually tissue histology/immunohistochemistry.
** Negative blood PCR does not exclude gastrointestinal disease or retinitis; investigate compatible symptoms.
* ''Primary prophylaxis:'' Letermovir for CMV-seropositive recipients (R+), regardless of donor serostatus.
** Start early, day 0–28; continue through day 100, extending through day 200 when high-risk.
** Routine letermovir is not recommended for R− recipients; use CMV-safe blood products.
* ''Letermovir:'' CMV-specific terminase inhibitor; blocks DNA processing/packaging and virion maturation.
** ''Dose:'' 480 mg PO/IV daily; 240 mg daily with cyclosporine.
** Minimal marrow/renal toxicity; no HSV/VZV coverage—continue acyclovir/valacyclovir prophylaxis as indicated.
** Review interactions, particularly calcineurin inhibitors/azoles; monitor interacting drug levels.
** No renal adjustment when creatinine clearance >10 mL/min; data insufficient at ≤10 mL/min/on dialysis.
*** IV vehicle can accumulate below 50 mL/min; monitor creatinine closely.
** Not a treatment drug: resistance emerges readily with active replication; polymerase-inhibitor cross-resistance is not expected.

!! Surveillance and treatment trigger

''D = donor; R = recipient; +/− = CMV IgG seropositive/seronegative before transplant.''

''Highest risk: R+ recipients.'' Donor positivity alone (D+/R−) carries lower risk than recipient positivity.

|!Donor / recipient|!CMV risk|!Prevention and surveillance|
|D−/R+|''Highest-risk serologic pairing'': recipient reactivation; greater risk of prolonged/recurrent infection.|''Letermovir'' plus at least weekly PCR through day 100, including during prophylaxis.|
|D+/R+|''High risk'': recipient reactivation; donor CMV immunity may reduce prolonged/recurrent infection relative to D−/R+.|''Letermovir'' plus at least weekly PCR through day 100, including during prophylaxis.|
|D+/R−|Intermediate risk: donor-derived primary infection; lower overall risk than R+ recipients.|At least weekly PCR through day 100; PET when indicated. ''No routine letermovir''.|
|D−/R−|Lowest risk: primary infection remains possible.|CMV-safe blood products; ''no routine letermovir''. Lower-intensity PCR surveillance may suffice per center protocol.|

* ''Across groups:'' Keep PCR assay/specimen type consistent; serostatus alone does not determine treatment thresholds.
** Extend surveillance at least three further months when high-risk; continue during substantial immunosuppression and after prophylaxis withdrawal.
** Additional risks: cord/haploidentical/mismatched graft, T-cell depletion, steroids, graft-versus-host disease (GVHD), prior DNAemia, delayed immune recovery.
* ''Threshold:'' Use the center's validated threshold, viral kinetics, prophylaxis status, and clinical risk.
** Reported plasma triggers span ~100–1,500 IU/mL; whole-blood values are not interchangeable.
** Rising loads/high-dose steroids favor earlier PET; repeat low-level results promptly to establish trajectory.
** ''Article example:'' 240 IU/mL resolved on letermovir without steroids; ~220 IU/mL would trigger treatment with ≥1 mg/kg corticosteroids.
*** These are historical risk-adapted examples, not universal contemporary cutoffs.
** Isolated low-level letermovir “blips” may reflect abortive replication; do not automatically stop prophylaxis.
** Suspected organ disease requires urgent evaluation/treatment independently of a blood-PCR threshold.

